Below is a **single-user home-lab MVP**: Mac runs Ollama, FastAPI, LangChain, and persistent Chroma; Pi serves the browser UI and proxies requests; Tailscale Serve makes both available only within your tailnet. There’s **no public VPS, router port-forwarding, or direct browser access to Ollama**. This is local-first, not air-gapped—the Mac and Pi need network connectivity for Tailscale.

The examples index PDF, TXT, Markdown, and DOCX files. LangChain’s history-aware chain helpers are currently documented in `langchain_classic`, so the setup installs that package explicitly. <citation src="11"></citation>

## 1. Set up Tailscale

Install Tailscale on the Mac, Pi, and the phone or computer you’ll use to access the UI. Sign them into the same tailnet. In the Tailscale admin console, restrict access to the devices and user you intend to use.

Tailscale Serve proxies a local service over HTTPS to your tailnet, and its access-control rules apply to the service. Don’t use Funnel or create router port-forwarding rules for this setup. <citation src="6,7"></citation>

## 2. Prepare the Mac

